nt  absolutely it's one of the  largest problems right now in the  deployment of renewables in the united  states  the middle of the united states the  planes are an incredible wind resource  the southwest is an incredible solar  resource but that's simply not where  people are where industry is and moving  the electricity that can be generated  there at extraordinarily low cost  to the coast where most people live it's  just incredibly difficult we don't have  the infrastructure right now  and we're really struggling to build it  what does the infrastructure have to  look like  well we're talking about our large uh  transmission lines uh  mostly dc i wrote about a group of  entrepreneurs that were trying to build  a direct current  line i mean this is what's happening in  china it's what's happening in germany  and the united states simply doesn't  have the same kind of transmission  infrastructure  to move power around and and that's  really going to be a problem going  forward in the united states because  it's going to be more difficult to get  access to the extraordinarily  inexpensive renewables and a bigger  grid means that the grid is more robust  and frankly it means it's it's more  protected  from things like a hack or or any kind  of other national security  intrusion but if the electricity itself  is inexpensive is investing in this kind  of distribution  infrastructure expensive it costs a lot  of money to build  transmission lines we're talking the  billions of dollars but every single  person who's looked at this  from from a bigger picture economic  point of view says that that investment  pays back many times over so let's talk  about investment obviously the biden  administration has outlined its  goals for long-term economic spending  kind of a question mark as to how much  of that will actually be realized  but from what you've seen does it go far  enough  it's a huge step in the direction of  creating incentives  for private industry to want to build  these transmission lines i mean one of  the biggest ideas is out there  is a transmission tax credit however  it's not just about money  to build a transmission line is  extraordinarily difficult it is  many times more difficult than to build  something like a natural gas  pipeline a similar piece of  infrastructure the united states makes  it so much easier to build  a buried pipeline in terms of eminent  domain in terms of  streamlining the process building  transmission is just extraordinarily  difficult  and it's not because we want to make it  more difficult  what can the u.s learn from other  countries in that respect  well i i think you know getting back to  this question of streamlining one of the  things that i think would be incredibly  important is that  right now um if you go to a place like  germany if you go to a place like china  if you want to build i mean obviously in  china there's there's no you know  it's it's state-run but in germany if  you want to build a transmission line  you have to meet certain environmental  certain social  hurdles you know you have to sort of  prove you know that the transmission  line is is  what will not disturb too many people  etc  but you have ultimately a regulator you  can go to  to get that answer in the united states  you don't  uh there's no one that will really give  you a final answer and that's why  the decisions get stretched out for five  or ten years well  when you're a private industry and  you're trying to get investors and  you're saying  well we really won't know if we can make  a go of this for five or ten years  that that's that's the kiss of death  it's it's not going to work and so what  the united states could learn from other  countries  is to have a process where if you want  to build transmission  here's what you can do and you'll get a  yes or no answer does this make sense is  this in the national interest  and you'll get it within a certain time  frame two or three years seems  reasonable to me  that's what the united states could  learn from other countries that are  doing this more successfully  all right russell gold author of  superpower one man's quest to transform  american energy thank you so much  coming up how do you bring people out of  poverty  without wrecking the climate in the  process we'll explore how one company is  helping  rural communities power themselves this  is bloomberg green  [Music]  from bloomberg's headquarters in new  york i'm kaylee lynes and this  is bloomberg green well a rural village  in kenya  might not be the first place you'd  expect to find electric motorcycles  internet service and thriving local  businesses  but a solar powered mini grid is making  it all possible  u.s base energy startup power hive is on  a mission to supply clean energy to  rural communities around the world  bringing economic opportunity without  the need for fossil fuels  in africa 650 million people don't have  electricity  don't have access to power don't have  access to an  industry people who would otherwise have  been very productive  if they'd had the means if they had the  capital  [Music]  it's really important that we don't lose  sight of these people  in our bid to grow our economies and  become more industrialized  one of the toughest problems in the  world is how do you bring people out of  extreme poverty  without wrecking the climate in the  process  conventional wisdom says it'll require  the burning of enough fossil fuels  to cancel out any progress the developed  world makes on emissions  but here in rural kenya a small  experiment is underway to see if it's  possible to have the economic uplift  without the environmental catastrophe  [Music]  power hive is the first privately  licensed utility in kenya  we currently operate about 25 village  level microgrids  we're generating our power from solar  we're storing that energy  in batteries when i started in this  business 20 years ago the price per watt  of a solar panel was five six dollars  now it's 20 cents  it's opened up a huge opportunity in  africa to deliver energy  a micro or mini grid like the ones built  by power hive  is a power system that supplies enough  electricity for a small community  schools and homes get electric light and  internet service  agricultural labor like maize milling  doesn't have to be done by hand anymore  electric pressure cookers eliminate the  need to chop down trees  or burn charcoal to make meals all of  these things raise the standard of  living  but there's one big problem when we  first came into kenya  we quickly realized that our consumers  are  too poor to pay for energy  [Music]  many grids have been around for a long  long time the problem is is that there  hasn't been an economic model to support  the long-term  operations of these grids it forced us  to have to think about how  we can support our customers in  generating enough  income that they can sustain themselves  and then also create a good energy  customer for us as a business  in most rural communities in kenya  subsistence farmers would keep maybe 20  chicken  and these would be primarily for eggs  they might sell or consume themselves  really  but electricity along with some startup  capital from power hive  has changed all that powered appliances  like heat lamps and incubators  can transform chicken rearing into a  profitable business  it's a virtuous cycle for power hive  they give their customers the money and  guidance to start  businesses that use their electricity  and then a portion of the income from  those businesses  pays for the electricity  [Music]  you also find the community members who  do see successful businesses  get inspired to do that themselves and  all of that wouldn't have happened  if they hadn't had the exposure or  understood how it can be done or that it  can be done  [Music]  recently power hive has invested in a  new business  leasing electric vehicles to people in  the villages and in nearby kisi town  my name is bryan adore i'm running the  ground operations for this ev project in  this region of kenya what we're trying  to do here  is convert all conventional bikes from  petrol  engines to electric motors  beyond just getting from place to place  motorbikes are key to generating income  in this region  where they're often used as taxi  vehicles or for transporting goods to  market  and while making the switch from gas to  electric is obviously cleaner  there are some more tangible benefits as  well  maintaining electric bikes is way more  cheaper than  maintaining a conventional bike in terms  of servicing  electric vehicles they have fewer moving  parts they don't require  oil you know here people are spending  around 10 us dollars every two weeks  for the maintenance costs so if we can  save somebody one or two dollars a day  it's a very very big deal it's still  early days for mini grid companies like  power hive  and it remains to be seen how big the  industry's impact can be  a study by bloomberg nef found that  electricity from solar hybrid mini grids  is still expensive for many rural  customers  and the sector suffers from a lack of  investment  meanwhile large-scale renewables are  booming  bulk solar and wind are the most  competitive new sources of energy in  most of the world today  but with hundreds of millions of people  still living beyond the reach of major  grids  there's a massive opportunity to improve  lives without the climate paying the  cost  our business model if it works you know  can really change  what development means and what we're  trying to do  is create a model that can be deployed  anywhere  in rural communities across the globe  we have hundreds of millions of people  that are going to be joining the modern  economy  and you know these customers cannot do  so in a carbon  unfriendly way and here in kenya  we're showing that you can sort of  create this model which  you know we hope is actually going to  serve africa for generations  that's it for this week's edition but  keep the conversation going  follow us on twitter at climate i'm  kaylee lines  and this is bloomberg green  you
 

